    "body": "Nobody asked for redaction. I added it, on the reasoning that \"these logs get pasted somewhere public during a connector mystery\" \u2014 a real risk at *paste* time, applied as permanent deletion at *write* time, on a single-tenant service whose only readers are the people who run it.\n\nThe staging deploy made both symptoms concrete:\n\n**GitHub was posting to `/webhooks/github` \u2014 plural, not a route \u2014 and every 404 said only that a POST happened.** Not the path with its query, not the body, not which of GitHub's own headers described the delivery. A week of \"Not Found\" answered nothing about what was being asked for.\n\n**An accepted delivery said `pull_request arrived` and nothing else.** Not the repository, the action, the PR number, or the target branch \u2014 the entire content of \"did the right thing happen\". The payload was sitting in Firestore, where nobody reading a log is looking.\n\n## The inversion\n\n`redact()` was an allowlist: four headers logged, everything else replaced with `<redacted>`. Now a denylist of what is actually a credential \u2014 `authorization`, `cookie`, `x-hub-signature-256` and their kin \u2014 and everything else is data about our own service.\n\nThe security argument for the allowlist was \"redact everything in case we are compromised\", which leaves nobody able to answer *what did they send* \u2014 the one question that matters afterwards. Forensics is the thing logs are for; the allowlist removed the evidence to protect it.\n\n`_path()` stops stripping the query. On a 404 the query is most of what the caller was trying to do.\n\n## What a refusal now records\n\nThe body, capped at 2KB, only when the request was refused (`code >= 400`). An accepted webhook payload is tens of KB and already in the store, so a copy per delivery is cost without an answer \u2014 but a refusal has nothing else, and \"what was that caller asking for\" is the whole question, whether it's GitHub pointed at the wrong path or somebody probing.\n\n## What an accepted delivery now records\n\n`webhooks.about()` reads four keys out of the verified payload \u2014 repository, action, number, target branch \u2014 onto the record. Reading them for a log line is not the cloud filtering on them: #59 puts routing downstream, and it stays there; this is answering \"what arrived\" without opening Firestore. Never the prose \u2014 a title or a body is someone's words, and `docs/structured-logging.md` already puts message content at TRACE and nowhere else.\n\n## Before / after\n\n```json\n// before\n{\"message\": \"pull_request\", \"verb\": \"pull_request\", \"peer\": \"github\", \"status\": 202}\n\n// after\n{\"message\": \"pull_request\", \"verb\": \"pull_request\", \"peer\": \"github\", \"status\": 202,\n \"repo\": \"danbarua/agent-bus\", \"action\": \"closed\", \"number\": 181, \"base\": \"main\"}\n```\n\n```json\n// before \u2014 a 404, answering nothing\n{\"message\": \"POST /webhooks/github\", \"status\": 404, \"path\": \"/webhooks/github\",\n \"headers\": {\"x-github-event\": \"<redacted>\", ...}}\n\n// after\n{\"message\": \"POST /webhooks/github\", \"status\": 404, \"path\": \"/webhooks/github?attempt=2\",\n \"headers\": {\"x-github-event\": \"pull_request\", ...},\n \"body\": \"{\\\"action\\\":\\\"closed\\\",\\\"repository\\\":{\\\"full_name\\\":\\\"danbarua/agent-bus\\\"},...}\"}\n```\n\n## Mutation testing\n\nSix mutants, each caught:\n\n| mutation | result |\n|---|---|\n| allowlist restored | fail |\n| a refusal records no body | fail |\n| every request copies its payload | fail |\n| the query stripped again | fail |\n| `authorization` made loggable | fail |\n| payload fields silently dropped | fail |\n\n`ci-build.sh` green \u2014 213 cloud tests, 0 type errors.\n\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_013zWFQQKgFbvcNASW6nXwTR",
